dubai isn't your average cosmopolitan  city  it's a lively place rich in culture and  steeped in history  and although it may be known for  architectural marvels  the city is complemented by an array of  landscapes  wildlife and natural wonders what sets  it apart  is its many unexpected surprises  the first location gives us a glimpse  into the city's history and beginnings  as a thriving trade hub  set in the spectacular desert landscape  the saruk al-hadid archaeological site  is one of the most exciting discoveries  of the 21st century  the site is considered to have been a  center of constant human habitation  trade and metallurgy dating back over  three thousand years to the iron age  unique artifacts discovered at the site  are on public display  at the world's only live museum the  soruk al-hadid archaeology museum allows  visitors to delve deeper into the story  of bustling  trade routes between some of the most  influential civilizations  regularly updated artifacts housed at  the museum include axe heads and daggers  to metal and stone jewelry  among them a 4 000 year old intricate  gold ring which inspired the logo of the  iconic expo 2020  the 3d cinematic room transports  visitors to the edge of the desert to  learn more about some of the fascinating  discoveries  our next spot is home to a huge variety  of feathered residents  [Music]  just inside the bustling city of dubai  you'll find a treasure trove of diverse  bird life  in the vast wetlands of the russell  corps wildlife sanctuary  known as the cape of the creek it  comprises a variety of salt flats  mangroves and natural lagoons that is  one of the few urban protected areas in  the region  from one of the three bird hides you can  witness more than 25  000 resident and migratory birds which  include  great egrets reef herons cormorants and  more  though the real stars of the show are  the pink flamingos that flock to the  area during the winter  in the hope of attracting a mate a leica  telescope sits at the mangrove hide on  the southern edge of the sanctuary  to watch the majestic raptors and  greater spotted eagles  soar overhead guided tours take visitors  on a journey to explore the wetlands  through the eyes of a conservationist  [Music]  our next location goes deep into the  water off dubai's coast  a short ride from dubai's glistening  shoreline lies a little-known  marine adventure the emerald waters of  the gulf were once known for pearl  diving  and bustling trade over 500 species of  aquatic life reside in the pristine  warm waters surrounding dubai these  waters are home to many  magical secrets of the underwater world  one of the most popular dive sites sits  at a depth of 30 meters  the zaynab a 70 meter long oil smuggling  vessel  bound for asia is a thrilling deep wreck  site for advanced  open water divers other interesting  wrecks include the sheikh mohammed barge  neptune 6 and victoria star where divers  can explore  many more mysteries that lie deep below  next we head into the desert to discover  diverse wildlife that thrives in one of  the largest conservation reserves in the  region  only half an hour from dubai and  spanning more than 40 hectares of  tranquil desert shrubland and wetlands  the el mar moon reserve is home to over  200 species of native birds  the name marmom is inspired by one of  its most revered occupants the hubara  bustard  an internationally protected species of  bird that nomadic civilizations relied  on heavily to survive in the arabian  deserts  tall observation towers like the nest  look out over 19 species of endangered  animals  the highest concentration of large  flamingos in a desert environment and  one of the largest arabian gazelle herds  in the uae  the almar moon bedouin experience takes  visitors on a historical adventure  to discover what life was like in the  9th century when nomadic bedouin  traversed the deserts  visitors can enjoy cultural performances  a falcon show  and delectable emirati cuisine  surrounded by the majesty  of the desert dunes  and last we travel through the desert in  the dark of the night  to experience the wonders of islamic  astronomy  [Music]  thousands of years ago to navigate the  vast dunes of dubai  bedouins used a technique involving the  sun and stars  instead of compasses they used their own  shadows as well as sand dunes that were  shaped by the wind to traverse the  desert  due to the constantly shifting landscape  the majority of their traveling was done  by night  when stars lit up the sky making it a  more trustworthy navigation source  the role of islamic astronomy  particularly from the 9th to 13th  centuries  still impacts modern navigation today  these ancient techniques and stories are  still shared in dubai  from the mountains of hatta to the dunes  of almar moon  the mesmerizing tales of the dancer a  ruckus  and the flying eagle altai air are but  just a few  settle in and let these legends  transport you  you
 

